window.docContents[115].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.CKA.Defs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___CKA___Defs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/CKA › Defs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 148 of 253 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (22)structure CKASchemeabbrev specabbrev AdversaryCompabbrev Adversarynotation \"Comp\"structure GameStatedef canSenddef canReceivedef allowCorrdef finisheddef afterSenddef afterReceivenotation \"GameM\"def sendImpldef sendWithAdversaryCoinsImpldef challImpldef receiveImpldef corrImpldef oracleImpldef initStatedef gamedef advantage\n\nContinuous key agreement (CKA): syntax and security gameTrusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file: read them. The theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Defines continuous key agreement (CKAScheme) and its synchronous ping-pong real-or-random security game (CKA.game, CKA.advantage), following ACD19 §4.1.1, Defs. 12–13 and Fig. 3. Odd epochs have A send and B receive; even epochs swap roles. A passive adversary may supply sender coins (sendWithAdversaryCoinsImpl) or corrupt states (corrImpl) outside the healing window around challenge epoch t* (allowCorr for max(t_A, t_B) ≤ t* − 2, or finished once t_P ≥ t* + Δ). Related CKA deviations appear in CKA/DDHDefs.lean (DEV-CKA9), Computational/Proofs/CryptoGames/CKA/DDH.lean (DEV-CKA8), and CKA/LibsignalCKA.lean (DEV-CKA10, DEV-CKA17).\n\n\n\nstructure CKASchemeL41–56 ↗#ACD19 Def. 12: a continuous-key-agreement (CKA) scheme with initialization keys Key, states State, sender coins Coins, messages Msg, and output keys CKey.\n\n\n\nabbrev specL66–72 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 oracle interface (none = refused).\n\nabbrev AdversaryCompL74–76 ↗#Computations with own coins plus the Fig. 3 CKA oracles.\n\nabbrev AdversaryL78–79 ↗#A CKA adversary outputting a hidden-bit guess.\n\n\n\nnotation \"Comp\"L85 ↗#\n\nstructure GameStateL89–102 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 game state.\n\n\n\ndef canSendL108–114 ↗#Ping-pong send condition: both parties are at the same epoch and P sends in the next epoch.DEV-CKA2 (formalization): req and ping-pong order. ACD19 states the ping-pong order in prose (§4.1.1) and aborts on a failing req. Here a call out of order, or with a failing req, returns none and leaves the state unchanged; the epoch counter grows only on success. Effect: none.\n\ndef canReceiveL116–118 ↗#Ping-pong receive condition: P.other is one epoch ahead of P.\n\ndef allowCorrL120–122 ↗#allow-corr :⇔ max(t_A, t_B) ≤ t* − 2.\n\ndef finishedL124–126 ↗#finished_P :⇔ t_P ≥ t* + Δ.\n\ndef afterSendL128–132 ↗#Update s after P sends T and transitions to state γ.\n\ndef afterReceiveL134–137 ↗#Update s after P receives and transitions to state γ.\n\n\n\nnotation \"GameM\"L148 ↗#\n\ndef sendImplL150–163 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 send-P: outside epoch t*, run CKA-S with fresh coins and return (T, I).DEV-CKA3 (formalization): no plain send in the challenge epoch. ACD19 Fig. 3 lets the adversary call send-P instead of chall-P in epoch t* (it then gets no challenge). Here send-P is refused in epoch t*, so that the DDH reduction can embed its challenge there. Effect: w.l.o.g., proved: CKA.advantagePaper_eq (Computational/Tests/Deviations/PaperFaithfulSendProof.lean) shows that every adversary of the paper's game has the same advantage as a converted adversary here.\n\ndef sendWithAdversaryCoinsImplL165–171 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 send-P'(r): run CKA-S with adversarial coins r while t_P + 1 ≤ t* − 2.\n\ndef challImplL173–185 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 chall-P: in epoch t*, run CKA-S with fresh coins and return (T, I) if b = false or (T, I') for uniform I' if b = true.\n\ndef receiveImplL187–200 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 receive-P: run CKA-R on lastMsg without revealing the derived key.DEV-CKA4 (formalization): receive-P outputs nothing, and the game does not end. As in ACD19 Fig. 3, the receiver's key is not given to the adversary. ACD19 §4.1.1 ends the game once both states are revealed after the challenge phase (\"not made explicit\"); here it continues, which cannot help the adversary. Effect: none.\n\ndef corrImplL202–205 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 corr-P: reveal γ_P if allowCorr or finished holds.\n\ndef oracleImplL207–210 ↗#Combined Fig. 3 oracle implementation.\n\n\n\ndef initStateL221–226 ↗#Initial state after Fig. 3 init for key k and hidden bit b.\n\ndef gameL228–239 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 3 hidden-bit game for challenge epoch t* and healing parameter Δ.DEV-CKA5 (formalization): t* and b. ACD19 Fig. 3 starts with init(t*). Here t* is a parameter of the game, and security quantifies over all t*. The hidden bit b = true means random (Fig. 3's b = 1). Effect: none.\n\ndef advantageL241–254 ↗#Adv^{CKA}_{ror,Δ}(A) (ACD19 Def. 13): the bias 2·|Pr[b = b'] − 1/2|.DEV-CKA7 (formalization): advantage as a bias. ACD19 §2.1 prints the advantage as 2·P[A wins] − 1/2, a typo. Lean uses the intended bias 2·|Pr[b = b'] − 1/2|, VCVio's boolBias. Effect: none.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/CKA/Defs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.CKA.Defs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___CKA___Defs"}});